Wait, I should double check the collision detection logic.
`ball.x` and `ball.y` are the center of the ball.
`ball.r` is the radius.
`brick.x`, `brick.y` are top-left. `brick.w`, `brick.h` are dimensions.

`collides(ball, brick)`:
`ball.x + ball.r > brick.x` (Ball's right edge is past brick's left edge)
`ball.x - ball.r < brick.x + brick.w` (Ball's left edge is before brick's right edge)
`ball.y + ball.r > brick.y` (Ball's bottom edge is past brick's top edge)
`ball.y - ball.r < brick.y + brick.h` (Ball's top edge is before brick's bottom edge)
This is correct for AABB.

Now the overlap:
`dx1 = ball.x + ball.r - brick.x` (Distance from brick's left edge to ball's right edge)
`dx2 = brick.x + brick.w - (ball.x - ball.r)` (Distance from ball's left edge to brick's right edge)
`dy1 = ball.y + ball.r - brick.y` (Distance from brick's top edge to ball's bottom edge)
`dy2 = brick.y + brick.h - (ball.y - ball.r)` (Distance from ball's top edge to brick's bottom edge)

If `dx1` is the smallest overlap, it means the ball's right edge just entered the brick's left edge -> hit from the left.
If `dx2` is smallest, it hit from the right.
If `dy1` is smallest, it hit from the top.
If `dy2` is smallest, it hit from the bottom.

Example: Ball moving right, hitting the left side of a brick.
`ball.x` is slightly less than `brick.x`.
`dx1 = ball.x + ball.r - brick.x` will be small.
`dx2 = brick.x + brick.w - (ball.x - ball.r)` will be large.
`dy1` and `dy2` will be larger than `dx1` (unless it's a corner hit).
So `minOverlapX < minOverlapY` will be true.
`dx1 < dx2` will be true.
`ball.vx = -ball.vx` (reflects to the left).
`ball.x = brick.x - ball.r` (pushes ball to the left of the brick).
